WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:02.600
“It’s time to bring our
people back home.”

00:00:02.600 --> 00:00:04.270
“...to end America’s
longest war.”

00:00:04.270 --> 00:00:08.890
“The US will soon complete its
withdrawal of forces from Afghanistan.”

00:00:08.890 --> 00:00:11.330
“Taliban fighters have
flooded the capital.”

00:00:11.330 --> 00:00:14.520
“There are American-made weapons
in the hands of Taliban fighters.”

00:00:14.520 --> 00:00:18.700
“Many of the Afghans trying to escape
actually helped American armed forces.”

00:00:18.700 --> 00:00:20.369
“It was quite clear that
it was going to come to this.”

00:00:20.369 --> 00:00:24.565
BIDEN: I stand squarely
behind my decision.

00:00:24.565 --> 00:00:31.040
After 20 years, there was never a
good time to withdraw US forces.

00:00:31.040 --> 00:00:33.239
AZMAT KHAN: People are
glued to their TV screens.

00:00:33.239 --> 00:00:35.140
It's chaotic.
It is a mess.

00:00:35.140 --> 00:00:42.239
But to really understand how we got here,
we have to go back to the beginning.

00:00:44.235 --> 00:00:48.480
So, for many Americans,
Afghanistan is the “good war.”

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:51.739
My name is Azmat Khan.
I’m an investigative reporter.

00:00:51.739 --> 00:00:55.260
They see the war in Iraq as
the one that was unjustified,

00:00:55.260 --> 00:00:57.320
going after something
that didn't exist,

00:00:57.320 --> 00:00:58.809
these weapons of
mass destruction.

00:00:58.809 --> 00:01:04.369
And they see Afghanistan as the place
that was shortchanged because of Iraq.

00:01:04.369 --> 00:01:07.579
Michael McKinley.
I was ambassador in Afghanistan.

00:01:07.579 --> 00:01:12.659
The decision to invade Afghanistan was
directly related to the attacks on 9/11.

00:01:12.659 --> 00:01:15.890
“Osama bin Laden publicly
declared war on the US.”

00:01:15.890 --> 00:01:19.180
“He openly bragged about his
direct involvement in planning 9/11.”

00:01:19.180 --> 00:01:22.180
AZMAT KHAN: Osama bin
Laden was in Afghanistan.

00:01:22.180 --> 00:01:26.048
BUSH: On my orders, the United States
military has begun strikes.

00:01:26.349 --> 00:01:29.131
AZMAT KHAN: But once the bombing
began, and in the lead-up to it,

00:01:29.131 --> 00:01:33.080
it was no longer just about,
we want to have bin Laden.

00:01:33.080 --> 00:01:35.691
It was about
the Taliban state.

00:01:35.691 --> 00:01:39.030
LAURA BUSH: The plight of
women and children in Afghanistan

00:01:39.030 --> 00:01:41.320
is a matter of deliberate
human cruelty.

00:01:41.320 --> 00:01:47.224
The fight against terrorism is also a fight
for the rights and dignity of women.

00:01:47.770 --> 00:01:52.662
AZMAT KHAN: There were actually offers
to turn over bin Laden by the Taliban.

00:01:52.662 --> 00:01:54.352
And the United States refused.

00:01:54.352 --> 00:01:58.610
They wanted a surrender
directly to the United States.

00:01:58.610 --> 00:02:04.619
Now, shortly after the bombing campaign
started, the Taliban fell fairly quickly.

00:02:04.619 --> 00:02:09.660
And by April of 2002, it was
very apparent to the Americans

00:02:09.660 --> 00:02:14.599
that they couldn't just sweep in,
overthrow a regime, and walk away.

00:02:14.599 --> 00:02:17.180
You needed to build
a new government.

00:02:17.180 --> 00:02:21.920
This nation-building program that
really prioritized counterterrorism.

00:02:22.259 --> 00:02:27.190
BUSH: We expect cells of
trained killers to try to regroup,

00:02:27.190 --> 00:02:31.152
and try to undermine Afghanistan's
efforts to build a lasting peace.

00:02:31.152 --> 00:02:34.159
AZMAT KHAN: This idea that, listen,
we're going to take the gloves off,

00:02:34.159 --> 00:02:37.942
and we're really going
to eradicate al-Qaeda.

00:02:42.688 --> 00:02:44.454
OBAMA: Good evening.

00:02:44.454 --> 00:02:48.610
Tonight, I can report to the
American people, and to the world,

00:02:48.610 --> 00:02:53.319
that the United States has conducted
an operation that killed Osama bin Laden,

00:02:53.319 --> 00:02:56.970
the leader of al Qaeda and
a terrorist who’s responsible

00:02:56.970 --> 00:03:02.618
for the murder of thousands of
innocent men, women, and children.

00:03:02.618 --> 00:03:06.720
MICHAEL MCKINLEY: The decrease in
troop presence really dates from 2011.

00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:12.220
There was a sense that, overall,
the threat of a terrorist attack

00:03:12.220 --> 00:03:17.530
against the United States from
Afghanistan was greatly diminished.

00:03:17.530 --> 00:03:21.900
Part of what American forces in
Afghanistan were doing became

00:03:21.900 --> 00:03:25.510
providing more formalized training,
building up equipment,

00:03:25.510 --> 00:03:29.489
providing intelligence support,
eventually providing air support...

00:03:29.489 --> 00:03:35.620
AZMAT KHAN: Afghan soldiers were supported
by US aircraft that would fire on insurgents.

00:03:35.620 --> 00:03:39.060
Really, it was just air power that
allowed the Afghan government

00:03:39.060 --> 00:03:44.430
to retain this tenuous hold in places where
they weren't necessarily very popular.

00:03:45.466 --> 00:03:48.230
MICHAEL MCKINLEY: The Afghan
security forces weren't evolving

00:03:48.230 --> 00:03:51.448
quite in the manner that was anticipated.

00:03:52.145 --> 00:03:57.430
AZMAT KHAN: At the same time, you also saw
billions of dollars in reconstruction money

00:03:57.430 --> 00:04:01.790
pouring into a country without the kind of
infrastructure to really handle that kind

00:04:01.790 --> 00:04:02.790
of money.

00:04:02.790 --> 00:04:04.840
That means building
health clinics.

00:04:04.840 --> 00:04:08.390
It means building
schools, bridges, roads.

00:04:08.390 --> 00:04:10.970
And more than that,
that kind of reconstruction money,

00:04:10.970 --> 00:04:13.090
coupled with America's
counterterrorism goals,

00:04:13.090 --> 00:04:15.900
was really
a toxic mix.

00:04:15.900 --> 00:04:19.630
Partnering with very corrupt
warlords, or local strongmen,

00:04:19.630 --> 00:04:24.250
who, in exchange for allowing you
to build a school on this land,

00:04:24.250 --> 00:04:27.870
you would give us the contract
for the health clinic right next door.

00:04:27.870 --> 00:04:33.290
I had US soldiers tell me things like, yeah,
he was a warlord, but he was our warlord.

00:04:33.290 --> 00:04:37.950
Most media coverage from Afghanistan
that Americans might hear

00:04:37.950 --> 00:04:42.442
often comes from urban cities,
particularly Kabul.

00:04:42.442 --> 00:04:44.150
“From Kabul,
Afghanistan.”

00:04:44.150 --> 00:04:47.220
“In the Afghan
capital, Kabul.”

00:04:47.220 --> 00:04:51.757
But across the rural countryside,
support for the Taliban gained ground,

00:04:51.757 --> 00:04:56.007
because of this massive
campaign of airstrikes, of bombings,

00:04:56.007 --> 00:05:00.919
much of which have had
high civilian death tolls.

00:05:01.578 --> 00:05:03.860
The Taliban had
been defeated.

00:05:03.860 --> 00:05:09.930
And yet the American presence in Afghanistan
brought them back from the dead.

00:05:11.361 --> 00:05:17.660
MICHAEL MCKINLEY: In the 2013-2014 period,
the Taliban began to regain its footing.

00:05:17.660 --> 00:05:21.530
It was clear that every year
they were making some advances,

00:05:21.530 --> 00:05:27.763
and they were operating primarily
in rural and depopulated areas.

00:05:27.763 --> 00:05:34.506
AZMAT KHAN: US intelligence was either
incredibly misrepresented or incredibly flawed.

00:05:34.506 --> 00:05:37.060
MICHAEL MCKINLEY: There was
this growing concern, for example,

00:05:37.060 --> 00:05:39.510
with what is commonly
called “ghost soldiers.”

00:05:39.510 --> 00:05:42.410
How many soldiers were
actually on the rolls?

00:05:42.410 --> 00:05:48.630
Was it 330,000? Or was it several
tens of thousands lower than that?

00:05:48.630 --> 00:05:51.800
And the real issue related to
whether soldiers felt they were

00:05:51.800 --> 00:05:54.650
fighting for a government
that represented them.

00:05:54.650 --> 00:05:58.020
AZMAT KHAN: It's talked about often
that the Afghan government,

00:05:58.020 --> 00:06:01.020
and the particularly
the president,

00:06:01.020 --> 00:06:04.690
was not coming to the
negotiating table with the Taliban.

00:06:04.690 --> 00:06:07.250
“President Trump ordering
a drawdown of troops.”

00:06:07.250 --> 00:06:10.911
“All US troops should leave
Afghanistan by the end of the year.”

00:06:10.911 --> 00:06:13.990
“A very unpopular war
here in the United States.”

00:06:13.990 --> 00:06:15.821
“President Joe Biden has
decided the United States

00:06:15.821 --> 00:06:18.264
has seen enough of
the war in Afghanistan.”

00:06:18.264 --> 00:06:21.650
AZMAT KHAN: So when the Biden
administration announced that

00:06:21.650 --> 00:06:25.130
they would be continuing
Trump's plan to withdraw,

00:06:25.130 --> 00:06:30.451
many Afghanistan watchers were
poised for the Taliban to gain ground.

00:06:30.451 --> 00:06:35.689
The United States, which has been
providing air power to Afghan forces,

00:06:35.689 --> 00:06:42.000
now, all of a sudden, that's no longer going to
be something that Afghan forces can rely on.

00:06:43.374 --> 00:06:47.510
We saw them sweep through districts
in places that were more rural

00:06:47.510 --> 00:06:51.617
and we saw an escalation
of that in May of 2021.

00:06:51.617 --> 00:06:56.380
But I don't think anyone expected it
to gain ground as quickly as it did.

00:06:56.380 --> 00:06:58.750
“It’s clear who is
now in charge.”

00:06:58.750 --> 00:07:00.840
“The Taliban is
sweeping the country.”

00:07:00.840 --> 00:07:04.060
“Here in Kabul, the city has
collapsed. There’s no real security.”

00:07:04.060 --> 00:07:08.050
“As the Taliban solidify their control
of the city and the country...”

00:07:08.050 --> 00:07:11.490
“...the Afghans who went above and beyond
to help American servicemembers

00:07:11.490 --> 00:07:13.036
are stuck in limbo.”

00:07:13.846 --> 00:07:17.190
AZMAT KHAN: A large reason that many
Afghans worked with the Americans,

00:07:17.190 --> 00:07:21.010
or other foreigners, was that many of them
wanted a better life for their families,

00:07:21.010 --> 00:07:25.860
and hoped that it would one day
lead to their being able to leave.

00:07:25.860 --> 00:07:30.710
And so, watching the Taliban
come as suddenly as it did,

00:07:30.710 --> 00:07:34.281
knowing what their
futures might be,

00:07:34.281 --> 00:07:40.069
it is a failure on the part
of the US government.

